  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Simplify $\\frac{3}{c(5d+1)}-\\frac{5}{5d+1}.$",
  "worked_solution": "In order to subtract the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. Here, we can put both fractions over a common denominator of $c(5d+1)$ if we multiply the top and bottom of the second fraction by $c:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{c(5d+1)}-\\frac{5}{5d+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{c(5d+1)}-\\frac{c}{c}\\cdot \\frac{5}{5d+1} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{c(5d+1)}-\\frac{5c}{c(5d+1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3-5c}{c(5d+1)} & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
